\section{Methodology}
For the remainder of the manuscript, we focus on two-sample clinical trials with a continuous Normal endpoint \note{(maybe will extend also to proportion, we'll see)} and known standard deviations $\sigma_C$ and $\sigma_T$. 
Let $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$ denote the true parameters of the treatment and control groups, respectively. The treatment contrast is defined as $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$,
where we assume without loss of generality that lower values of $\theta$ represent a better clinical response, thus $\delta > 0$ indicates a treatment benefit. 

We evaluate the one-sided hypothesis test:
$$
H_0: \delta \le q \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1: \delta > q
$$

where $q$ denotes the reference value defining the null hypothesis, for instance $q = 0$ for a superiority test or a non-inferiority margin $q < 0$.

In the Bayesian framework, the trial design evaluates this hypothesis using a posterior decision rule.
Let $\mathcal{D} = (\bar{y}_T, \bar{y}_C)$ denote the observed trial data.
The trial is declared a success (i.e., $H_0$ is rejected) if the posterior probability of $\delta$ exceeding $q$ surpasses a pre-specified threshold $p$ (typically $p = 1 - \alpha$):
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:an_prior}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge p
\end{equation}
where $\pi_A$ explicitly denotes the analysis prior used to derive the posterior distribution.  

\textbf{Running example.} 
To fix ideas, throughout this section we illustrate all results with the following two-arm trial: $n_T = 40$ treatment patients, $n_C = 20$ control patients, and known standard deviation $\sigma = 88$ (lower values are better), giving sampling variances $v_C = 88^2/20$ and $v_T = 88^2/40$. 
Success is declared when $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\,\pi_A) \ge 0.95$. The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ is derived in Section~\ref{subsec:formulating_priors} from six historical placebo arms. The full design and its operating characteristics are presented in Section~\ref{sec:application1}.

\subsection{Formulating the MAP Prior}
\label{subsec:formulating_priors}

The Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior is derived from a hierarchical model that uses the exchangeability assumption across
historical trials \citep{schmidli_robust_2014}. Let $H$ denote the number of historical studies, with observed
control mean $\bar{y}_{h}$ and known sampling variances $v_h = \sigma^2/n_h$ for $h = 1,\ldots,H$. The model is:
\begin{align*}
    \bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h &\sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_h,\, v_h) \\
    \theta_h,\, \theta_C \mid \mu, \tau &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mu,\, \tau^2)
\end{align*}
with hyperpriors $p(\mu)$ and $p(\tau)$ placed on the population mean and between-study standard deviation. The heterogeneity
parameter $\tau$ governs the degree of borrowing: $\tau \approx 0$ implies all studies share a common control mean (full pooling),
while large $\tau$ signals large between-trial variation and justifies reduced borrowing.

The MAP prior for the new trial's control parameter $\theta_C$ is the marginal posterior predictive distribution given the historical data:
\begin{equation*} 
p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \bar{y}_1,\ldots,\bar{y}_H\right)
    \propto \int\!\int p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \mu,\tau\right)
    \left[\prod_{h=1}^{H} \int p(\bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h)\, p(\theta_h \mid \mu,\tau)\, d\theta_h\right]
    p(\mu)\,p(\tau)\; d\mu\; d\tau
\end{equation*}
This integral is not available in closed form and is evaluated via MCMC. The resulting posterior samples of $\theta_C$ are
then used to fit a $K$-component mixture of Normal distributions
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,\mu_k,\,\sigma_k^2)
\end{equation*}
chosen by minimizing the KL divergence of the mixture from the true MAP predictive. This conjugate mixture
approximation enables closed-form Bayesian updating: given the current trial mean $\bar{y}_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$, each
Normal component is updated independently via the standard Normal-Normal formula, and the posterior is again a Normal mixture with adjusted weights.

The Robust MAP prior augments $\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}$ with a weakly informative component to protect against prior-data conflict:
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{Robust}}(\theta_C)
    = (1 - \omega)\,\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) + \omega\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,m_R,\,\sigma_R^2)
\end{equation*}
where $\omega \in [0,1]$ is the prior probability that the new trial is not exchangeable with the historical controls, and $m_R$,
$\sigma_R^2$ are the location and variance of the vague component.
The borrowing is dynamic because the mixture weights are themselves updated by the data. After observing the control data $\bar{y}_C$, the posterior weight on the vague component becomes \citep{neuenschwander_fixed_2023}
\begin{equation*}
    \tilde{\omega} = \frac{\omega\, m_R(\bar{y}_C)}{\omega\, m_R(\bar{y}_C) + (1 - \omega)\, m_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\bar{y}_C)},
\end{equation*}
where $m_j(\bar{y}_C) = \int \pi_j(\theta_C)\, f(\bar{y}_C \mid \theta_C)\, d\theta_C$, for $j \in \{\mathrm{MAP},\, R\}$, is the marginal likelihood of the data under each component. Under prior-data conflict the vague component explains the observed data better, $m_R(\bar{y}_C) > m_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\bar{y}_C)$, so $\tilde{\omega} > \omega$ and the posterior down-weights the historical component. The design therefore borrows most when prior and data agree and least when they conflict, limiting the influence of misleading historical information.
Following \citet{schmidli_robust_2014}, throughout this thesis we set the vague component to a unit-information prior (UIP): a Normal with variance $\sigma_R^2 = \sigma^2$ and $m_R$ equal to the historical mean, corresponding to an effective sample size of one observation, which is weakly informative yet proper.

The tuning of $\omega$, $m_R$ and $\sigma_R^2$ is not trivial in general:
\citet{ratta2026interplay} show that Lindley's paradox (that is, even though a very dispersed robust component might not seem influential, it will result in a high posterior weight being assigned to the informative component) and asymptotic T1E inflation can be avoided only when $\omega$ and $\sigma_R^2$
are calibrated jointly rather than independently. Instead, \citet{weru_information_2026} demonstrate that a heavier-tailed vague component or a data-dependent location can more
effectively cap T1E rates under severe drift. Regarding $\omega$ itself, \citet{neuenschwander_fixed_2023}
argue that it does not need to be assigned a hyperprior: even a fixed weight is implicitly updated by the current data through the mixture
posterior weights, so a Beta prior on $\omega$ adds little beyond additional complexity.


\textbf{Running example.}
To obtain the MAP prior, we follow the worked example at \url{https://opensource.nibr.com/RBesT/articles/introduction_normal.html}. 
Particularly, we fit the hierarchical model via MCMC by assuming a weakly informative Normal on $\mu$ centred at zero with scale $\sigma$, and a
HalfNormal on $\tau$ with scale $\sigma/2$, a weakly informative choice that places most prior mass on small between-trial heterogeneity. Then, the package exploits EM algorithm to select the number of components $K$ automatically by AIC.

Applying these steps to the six historical placebo arms yields the hierarchical model fit shown in Figure~\ref{fig:map_forest}, and Figure~\ref{fig:map_robust_plot} compares the resulting single-component Normal approximation with the 3-component mixture selected by AIC and its robustified version. 
Each prior is summarised by its effective sample size (ESS), roughly the number of concurrent control patients it is worth \citep{neuenschwander2020predictively}. Because the ESS depends on the shape of the approximating prior, the single Normal, the mixture and the robust mixture summarise the same MAP predictive with different values (respectively $20$, $40$ and $29$). 
Throughout this section we adopt the single-component Normal as the analysis prior of the running example, since it makes the closed-form results of the following sections tractable. The full 3-component mixture and its robust extension are carried forward to the application in Section~\ref{sec:application1}.
<<map_setup, include=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
library(RBesT)
library(ggplot2)
crohn_sigma <- 88
dat <- crohn
dat$y.se <- crohn_sigma / sqrt(dat$n)
set.seed(1)
map_mcmc <- gMAP(cbind(y, y.se) ~ 1 | study,
  weights = n, data = dat,
  family = gaussian,
  beta.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma),
  tau.dist = "HalfNormal", tau.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma / 2)
)
map_fit_1 <- automixfit(map_mcmc, Nc = 1)   # single Normal
map_fit_3 <- automixfit(map_mcmc)            # full mixture: selected by AIC
ess_map_1 <- round(ess(map_fit_1))
ess_map_3 <- round(ess(map_fit_3))

map_robust <- robustify(map_fit_3, weight = 0.2, mean = -50, sigma = crohn_sigma)
ess_map_rob <- round(ess(map_robust))

@

<<map_forest, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Forest plot of the hierarchical meta-analysis fit. For each historical placebo arm, the observed control mean $\\bar{y}_{C,h}$ with 95\\% interval is shown alongside the posterior mean and 95\\% credible interval of $\\theta_h$. The bottom row (\\textit{MAP}) is the posterior predictive for the new trial's $\\theta_C$.">>=
forest_plot(
  map_mcmc,
  prob       = 0.95,      # 95% credible intervals
  est        = "MAP",    # show only the MAP predictive row (theta_C)
  model      = "both",  # show both raw (stratified) and shrunken (meta) per-study estimates
  point_est  = "median",  # posterior median as point estimate
  size       = 1.25,
  linewidth  = 1.25
) + ylab(expression(theta[C])) #for some reason they made the plot inverted x and y axis 
@

<<map_robust_plot, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap=paste0("MAP prior approximations and robust extension. The 1-component Normal (ESS $= ", ess_map_1, "$, used in the running example) and the 3-component mixture selected by AIC (ESS $= ", ess_map_3, "$) both approximate $\\pi_{\\mathrm{MAP}}$. Adding a weakly informative UIP component ($\\mathcal{N}(-50,\\,88^2)$ with weight $\\omega = 0.2$ yields the Robust MAP (ESS $= ", ess_map_rob, "$).")>>=
theta_grid <- seq(-300, 180, length.out = 600)
labels <- c("1-component MAP", "3-component MAP", "Robust MAP")
# legend labels as plotmath so that omega renders as the symbol
legend_labels <- c(
  "1-component MAP",
  "3-component MAP",
  "Robust MAP"
)
df_map <- rbind(
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_1,  theta_grid), prior = labels[1]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_3,  theta_grid), prior = labels[2]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_robust, theta_grid), prior = labels[3])
)
df_map$prior <- factor(df_map$prior, levels = labels)
ggplot(df_map, aes(x = theta, y = density, colour = prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.9) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#0072B2", "#D55E00", "#009E73"), labels = legend_labels) +
  labs(x = expression(theta[C]), y = "Density", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-150, 70)) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
@




\subsection{Metrics to evaluate Bayesian Designs}
\label{subsec:metrics_bayesian}
Frequentist T1E and power are well-established metrics used to evaluate traditional trial designs. However, it is documented that when informative priors are incorporated into a 
design, strict point-wise control of the frequentist T1E is impossible \citep{viele_use_2014, kopp2024simulating}. Consequently, in the Bayesian framework, alternative metrics are often evaluated, such 
as Assurance \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005} or Predictive Power \citep{micheloud_power_2022, spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986}.



All these metrics are special cases of a common metric $m$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}, which can be expressed as the expected probability of study success, integrated over a 
specified prior distribution $\pi_D$ for a parameter $\theta$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_general}
m = \int \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta\right) \pi_D(\theta) \, d\theta
\end{equation}


It is crucial to differentiate between design and analysis prior \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta)$ is the distribution used during the actual analysis of the trial, to derive the posterior and evaluate the decision rule (see Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}). In this manuscript, 
$\pi_A(\theta)$ incorporates historical data via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior, but other methods can be employed (see Section~\ref{subsec:info-borrowing})
  \item  The design prior $\pi_D(\theta)$, (see Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general}) is the assumed distribution of the underlying true parameter (data generating process) used exclusively in the planning stage to evaluate the design's 
operating characteristics across various plausible true scenarios.
\end{itemize}
While the approach of using the exact same prior beliefs for both the data generation and the inference ($\pi_D = \pi_A$) 
is considered the most logically consistent and natural by many Bayesian 
methodologists \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}, it may be not sufficient to regulatory authorities. In fact, a more comprehensive sensitivity analysis can be required, in order to evaluate the 
design under "prior-data conflict" or "drift" scenarios, where the prior information used for analysis ($\pi_A$) differs from the true data generating process ($\pi_D$). 

Historical information can be borrowed by placing a prior either on the control group $\theta_C$ (known in the literature as hybrid-control trials) or directly on the treatment contrast $\delta$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}. We focus on borrowing on the control group. Section~\ref{subsec:contrast_limitation} explains why the metrics developed below, do not extend straightforwardly to the treatment contrast.




\phantomsection\label{sec:pointwise}
When historical borrowing is applied exclusively on the control arm, the operating characteristics depend jointly on the true parameters $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$. The general metric from Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general} expands
 to a double integral over the joint design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T)$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_joint}
m = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T\right) \pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T) \, d\theta_T \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}
The traditional definitions of both T1E and Power impose deterministic constraints 
on the relationship between the treatment and control parameters. Specifically, we can fix the treatment 
contrast at a constant value $\delta$, such that $\theta_T = \theta_C - \delta$. Therefore, the joint design prior is reduced to a marginal prior on the control group, 
$\pi_D(\theta_C)$, collapsing Equation~\ref{eq:m_joint} into a single integral, and by varying the assumed true effect size $\delta$, we can define two metrics for evaluating the trial design:

\paragraph{Average T1E (avgT1E)}
When the true treatment effect is null ($\delta = 0$), we obtain the Average T1E \citep{best_beyond_2025}:  
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avg-type1-error}
  \avgTIE = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}
\Remark By defining $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta function centered at a specific value $\theta_C^*$, Equation~\ref{eq:avg-type1-error} collapses 
to the classic frequentist T1E at $\theta_C^*$.  It is easy to notice that, in this case,
the conditional T1E is not a single constant, but varies pointwise as a function of $\theta_C^*$. 


\paragraph{Average Power (avgPow)}
When the true treatment effect is assumed to be a specific non-zero value ($\delta \neq 0$),  we obtain the Average Power (at $\delta$):  
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avg-power}
  \avgPow(\delta) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}
\Remark We use the term "Average Power" rather than "Assurance" for Equation~\ref{eq:avg-power} to avoid confusion. Assurance often 
implies marginalization over a prior distribution for the effect size $\delta$ \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}. Because our framework fixes 
$\delta$ and integrates solely over the control parameter space $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, "Average Power" prevents ambiguity.


%% As an alternative, it is also proposed to compute the maximum (or minimum) operating characteristic over a plausible subset of the parameter space, 
%% denoted as $\Theta_C$. Then this restricted maximum T1E is expressed as:
%% $$\max_{\theta_C \in \Theta_C} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right)$$

\paragraph{Analytical results under Normal Priors}

Assuming Normal distributions for both the historical analysis prior ($\pi_A$) and the design prior ($\pi_D$), it is possible to derive closed-form solutions for Equation~\eqref{eq:avg-power} and ~\eqref{eq:avg-type1-error}. 
Complete proofs are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}. 

Let the observed sample means for the control and treatment arms be normally distributed as $\bar{y}_C \mid \theta_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$ and $\bar{y}_T \mid \theta_T \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_T, v_T)$, respectively, with $v_{\bullet} = \sigma_{\bullet}^{2}/n_{\bullet}$. 
We incorporate an informative historical analysis prior for the control arm, $\pi_A(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{A,C}, \sigma_{A,C}^2)$, where $\mu_{A,C}$ represents the historical mean of control group and $\sigma_{A,C}^2$ quantifies the prior uncertainty. We assume a non-informative analysis prior for the treatment arm.
The Bayesian Trial is declared a success if $\Pr(\delta > q \mid \bar{y}_C, \bar{y}_T) > p$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}.

Let $\hat{\delta}$ denote the posterior mean of the treatment contrast ($\delta$). By Normal-Normal conjugacy, the posterior mean of the control arm is a convex combination governed by $W = \sigma_{A,C}^2 / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C)$. Consequently, we can rewrite the condition of Success in the form: 

\begin{equation} \label{eq:threshold_k}
    \hat{\delta} > k \qquad \text{with } k = q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}
\end{equation}
where $q$ is the target efficacy threshold, $z_p$ is the Normal quantile corresponding to the required posterior probability, and $V_{\delta} = \sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C) + v_T$ is the posterior variance of $\delta$.

\paragraph{Conditional metrics}

By treating the true parameters as fixed constants ($\theta_C = \theta_C^*$ and $\theta_T = \theta_C^* - \delta$) and evaluating the frequentist sampling distribution of the estimator across repeated trials, we obtain the closed-form solutions for the conditional metrics. 

At a fixed $\theta_C$, the estimator $\hat{\delta}$ has sampling variance $V_{\text{samp}} = W^2 v_C + v_T$; this is distinct from the posterior variance $V_{\delta}$, which enters only through the decision threshold $z_p\sqrt{V_{\delta}}$. The Conditional Power, evaluated at a specific true control rate $\theta_C^*$ and true effect size $\delta$, is given by:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:power_closed_form}
    \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{samp}}}} \right)
\end{equation}
The conditional T1E is simply the special case evaluated under the strict null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$):
\begin{equation} \label{eq:t1e_closed_form}
    \TIE(\theta_C^*) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*)}{\sqrt{V_{\text{samp}}}} \right)
\end{equation}
Note that as $W \to 1$ (a non-informative analysis prior, or an infinitely large control group), the bias term vanishes and the formulation collapses to the classical two-sample $Z$-test power \citep{lachin1981introduction}:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:ztest_power}
    \Pow_Z(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\!\left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma\sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Averaged Metrics}
 When using a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{D,C}, \sigma_{D,C}^2)$, we apply the Laws of Total Expectation and Total Variance to marginalize out the unknown parameter $\theta_C$. 

Marginalizing over $\pi_D$ inflates the estimator's variance to the marginal variance $V_{\text{marg}} = W^2 v_C + v_T + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2 = V_{\text{samp}} + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$, which reduces to $V_{\text{samp}}$ when $\sigma_{D,C} \to 0$ (the conditional, or Dirac, case). The closed-form solution for the Average Power is:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_closed_form}
    \avgPow(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C}) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right)
\end{equation}
Evaluating this distribution under the null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$) yields the Average T1E:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}
    \avgTIE = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right)
\end{equation}
Consistent with the degenerate-prior view above, the conditional formulas of Equations~\eqref{eq:power_closed_form} and~\eqref{eq:t1e_closed_form} are exactly the limit of Equations~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} and~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form} as $\sigma_{D,C} \to 0$ with $\mu_{D,C} = \theta_C^*$: the term $(1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$ vanishes from the denominator and the design mean $\mu_{D,C}$ reduces to the fixed point $\theta_C^*$.

\paragraph{Observations on Averaged Metrics}
\begin{itemize}
    \item  The denominator of the averaged metrics includes the term $(1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$. This accounts for the additional uncertainty of integrating over $\pi_D$. Because dividing by a larger variance
     shrinks a $Z$-score toward zero, the larger marginal variance forces average metrics closer to $50\%$ compared to conditional ones (evaluated at $\mu_{D,C}$).
    \item  When the analysis prior and design prior coincide ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma_{D,C}^2$) and the null threshold is $q = 0$, the bias term vanishes and, as shown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}, the marginal variance equals the posterior variance ($V_{\text{marg}} = V_\delta$) and we obtain:
     \begin{align*}
          \avgPow(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right) \\
          &= 1 - \Phi\left( z_p - \frac{\delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right)
     \end{align*} 
     thus, the avgT1E is controlled at exactly $1-p$, aligning with the literature \citep{best_beyond_2025}. 
    \item When $W \to 1$, the formulation collapses to the frequentist two sample Z test.
    \item \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluates the case of using a completely vague design prior. This leads the marginal variance in the denominator to diverge to infinity. Consequently, both the Average Power
    and the Average T1E converge to $\Phi(0) = 0.5$, no matter the choice of the analysis prior.
\end{itemize}



\textbf{Running example.} 
In the figures below, the analysis prior is held fixed to the 1 component MAP prior ($\mu_{A,C} = -50$, $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = 88^2/20$) while the design prior parameters are varied to represent different assumptions about the true data generating process.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_t1e} visualizes how T1E reacts to two types of conflict between analysis and design priors: location shift ($\mu_{A,C} \neq \mu_{D,C}$) and scale shift ($\sigma_{A,C} \neq \sigma_{D,C}$). The left panel shows that if the true placebo response is better than expected ($\mu_{D,C} < -50$), the T1E increases. The conditional T1E (conditioned on $\theta_C^* = \mu_{D,C}$) lies below the avgT1E throughout the plotted range. In the right panel, when design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ is zero we obtain the conditional T1E, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$ the Average T1E converges to $0.5$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}

<<plot_t1e, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows T1E under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$), assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$. Conditional metric is evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$. Right panel shows Average T1E under Scale Shift (varying $\\sigma_{D,C}$) while setting $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$). The red dashed line represents the nominal value of $0.05$">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(gridExtra)
library(scales)

# --- Trial Parameters & Functions  ---
sigma <- 88; n_C <- 20; n_T <- 40; n_A <- 20; N_total <- n_C + n_T
v_C <- sigma^2 / n_C; v_T <- sigma^2 / n_T; sigma_A2 <- sigma^2 / n_A; mu_A <- -50
q <- 0; z_p <- qnorm(0.95)
W <- sigma_A2 / (sigma_A2 + v_C)
V_delta <- W * v_C + v_T
V_samp <- W^2 * v_C + v_T
k_SC <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta)   # SC (significance) threshold

# rejection probabilities for a generic threshold k
# (SC: k = k_SC; dual criterion: k = max(DV, k_SC))
condPower <- function(theta_C_star, delta, k)
  1 - pnorm((k - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - theta_C_star) + delta)) / sqrt(V_samp))
avgPower <- function(mu_D, sigma_D2, delta, k)
  1 - pnorm((k - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - mu_D) + delta)) / sqrt(V_samp + (1 - W)^2 * sigma_D2))
calc_EUII <- function(power, t1e, N) {
  eps <- 1e-10
  p <- pmax(pmin(power, 1 - eps), eps); t <- pmax(pmin(t1e, 1 - eps), eps)

  log_dor <- qlogis(p) - qlogis(t)
  return(exp(log_dor / N))
} 

my_theme <- theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())
my_x_scale <- scale_x_continuous(trans = "log1p", breaks = c(0, 10, 100, 1000, 10000), labels = c("0\n(Cond.)", "10", "100", "1K", "10K"))

# --- T1E Data & Plots ---
df_t1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional T1E` = condPower(mu_D, 0, k_SC),
    `Average T1E` = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional T1E`, `Average T1E`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

sigmas <- c(0, 10^seq(0, 5, length.out = 99))
df_t1e_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_T1E = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0, k_SC))

p1a <- ggplot(df_t1e_loc, aes(x = mu_D, y = Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average T1E" = "#E69F00", "Conditional T1E" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "T1E") +
  my_theme

p1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_T1E)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 0.6)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgT1E") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p1a, p1b, ncol = 2)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} shows the behavior of statistical power assuming $\mu_{D,C} = \mu_{A,C} = -50$. In the left panel $\sigma_{D,C} = \sigma_{A,C}$ and we vary $\delta$. As already observed, Average Power is always closer to $50\%$ than Conditional Power. The right panel shows how avgPower at $\delta = 60$ changes as design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ increases: when $\sigma_{D,C} = 0$ we obtain the conditional power, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$, avgPower reaches $50\%$.

<<plot_power, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="Left panel shows Conditional and Average Statistical Power evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows Average Power evaluated at $\\delta = 60$ as design uncertainty ($\\sigma_{D,C}$) increases, holding $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- Power Data & Plots ---
df_pow_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC),
    `Average Power` = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_Power = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 60, k_SC))

p2a <- ggplot(df_pow_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = "#E69F00", "Conditional Power" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Power") +
  my_theme

p2b <- ggplot(df_pow_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_Power)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0.4, 1.0)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgPower") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p2a, p2b, ncol = 2)
@

\paragraph{Sample size computation}
When performing sample size computation, it is reasonable to assume a correctly specified analysis prior ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C} = \sigma_{D,C}$) and a common patient variance $\sigma^2$ across both arms, such that $v_C = \sigma^2/n_C$ and $v_T = \sigma^2/n_T$.
We can also define the effective sample size (ESS) for the analysis prior $n_A$ ($n_A = n_D$ under the correctly specified prior assumption), and write $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma^2/n_A$.

Under these assumptions, the weight becomes $W = n_C/(n_C+n_A)$ and the bias term $(1-W)(\mu_{A,C}-\mu_{D,C})$ vanishes entirely. We further define two ratios:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $r_A = n_A/n_C$: the historical-to-control ESS ratio (implying $W = 1/(1+r_A)$).
    \item $r_T = n_T/n_C$: the allocation ratio.
\end{itemize}


Substituting the ESS parameterization into Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} and simplifying yields:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_ess}
    \avgPow(\delta) = 1 - \Phi \left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma \sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right)
\end{equation}
\Remark Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} is identical to the Power of a two sample Z-test (Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power}) evaluated with $n_C^* = n_C + n_A$ effective control patients.

Setting Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} equal to the target power $1-\beta$ and fixing the allocation ratio $r_T$ (so $n_T = r_T n_C$) yields:
$$ \frac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \frac{1}{r_T\, n_C} = \frac{(\delta - q)^2}{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1-\beta})^2} =: \frac{1}{K} $$
This results in a quadratic equation in terms of $n_C$:
$$ r_T\, n_C^2 + \bigl[r_T n_A - K(r_T + 1)\bigr]\, n_C - K n_A = 0 $$
The positive root provides the closed-form solution for the required control sample size:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:n_c_formula}
    n_C = \frac{K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A + \sqrt{\bigl[K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A\bigr]^2 + 4\, r_T\, K\, n_A}}{2\, r_T}, \quad K = \frac{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1 -\beta})^2}{(\delta - q)^2}
\end{equation}
As $n_A \to 0$ (no historical borrowing), Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} converges to $n_C \to K(1 + 1/r_T)$, which is exactly the classical frequentist two-sample $Z$-test formula with unequal group sizes. 


Average power is therefore a more natural metric for sample size planning in this Bayesian framework. Under the alignment assumptions the two variance terms coincide ($V_\text{marg} = V_\delta$), 
which yields two practical advantages. First, the required sample size follows in closed form from Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula}. Second, the historical sample size $n_A$ acquires a clean additive interpretation: it enters Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} only through the sum $n_C + n_A$, so each historical pseudo-observation reduces the required number of control patients by exactly one.
Instead, solving $\text{Conditional Power} = 1-\beta$ for $n_C$ requires $z_p\sqrt{V_\delta} + z_{1-\beta}\sqrt{V_\text{samp}} = \delta - q$, where both $n_C$ and $n_A$ enter through $V_\delta$ and $V_\text{samp}$ in different ways. 
The sample size can then only be obtained by numerical inversion, and $n_A$ has no convenient interpretation.


\textbf{Running example.} Using the parameters from the running example ($\sigma = 88$, $n_A = n_D = 20$, $r_T = 2$, $\delta = 60$, $q = 0$, $p = 0.95$), we obtain for a target power of 80\%:
$$ K = \frac{88^2 \times (1.645 + 0.842)^2}{60^2} \approx 13.30 $$
Substituting into Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} gives $n_C \approx 11.5 \to 12$, so $n_C = 12$ control patients ($n_T = 24$, $n_{\text{trial}} = 36$) achieves just above $80\%$ average power. The actual design ($n_C = 20$) yields $\avgPow(60) \approx 92\%$, consistent with Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} and with Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power} evaluated at $n_C^* = 40$.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_avgpower_nc} shows how average power varies with $n_C$ for several values of $n_A$: borrowing more historical data shifts the power curve upwards, reducing the required $n_C$. 

<<plot_avgpower_nc, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=3.5, fig.width=6, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average power varying control sample size $n_C$ at $\\delta = 60$ for varying $n_A$. The horizontal dashed line represents 80\\% power, while the two dotted verticals the minimum required $n_C$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:n_c_formula} with $n_A=20$) and actual design value $n_C = 20$.">>=
avgPower_ess <- function(delta, n_C, n_A, r_T, sigma, z_p, q = 0) {
  n_T <- r_T * n_C
  1 - pnorm(z_p - (delta - q) / (sigma * sqrt(1 / (n_C + n_A) + 1 / n_T)))
}

n_C_seq <- seq(4, 40, by = 0.5)
n_A_vals <- c(0, 10, 20, 30)
df_ss <- do.call(rbind, lapply(n_A_vals, function(na) {
  data.frame(
    n_C   = n_C_seq,
    power = avgPower_ess(delta = 60, n_C = n_C_seq, n_A = na,
                         r_T = 2, sigma = 88, z_p = qnorm(0.95)),
    n_A   = factor(paste0("n_A = ", na))
  )
}))

#
K_req   <- 88^2 * (qnorm(0.95) + qnorm(0.80))^2 / 60^2 # 13.30
b_req   <- K_req * (2 + 1) - 2 * 20
n_C_req <- (b_req + sqrt(b_req^2 + 4 * 2 * K_req * 20)) / (2 * 2)  # 11.5

legend_labels <- c("n_A = 0", "n_A = 10", "n_A = 20", "n_A = 30")

ggplot(df_ss, aes(x = n_C, y = power, colour = n_A)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.8, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = n_C_req, linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 20,      linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  annotate("text", x = n_C_req - 0.6, y = 0.15, label = paste0("n_C=", round(n_C_req, 1)),
           hjust = 1, size = 3, colour = "grey30") +
  annotate("text", x = 20 + 0.6, y = 0.15, label = "n_C=20",
           hjust = 0, size = 3, colour = "grey30") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 1), limits = c(0, 1)) +
  scale_colour_brewer(palette = "Set1", labels = legend_labels) +
  labs(x = expression(n[C]), y = "Average Power",
       colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  my_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "right")
@


\paragraph{Computational Framework \note{TO DO: decide wheter to keep or not: it is just how the metric is computed in R}}
We will now define the computational framework for the average metric, obtained extending the \texttt{RBesT} package of \citet{weber_applying_2021}.

Given $y_T$ and $y_C$ the sample means for the treatment and control arms, respectively. 

Success is determined by a decision function $D(y_T, y_C) \in \{0, 1\}$, which evaluates whether the posterior distribution of the treatment contrast, 
  $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, satisfies a set of predefined conditions. For instance, a standard design requires the posterior probability to 
  exceed a specific threshold: $\Pr(\delta > q \mid y_T, y_C) > p$ for threshold $q$ and probability $p$.

When evaluating multiple conditions simultaneously, such as in Dual-Criterion designs requiring both statistical significance and clinical relevance 
\citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, the decision is formulated as the product of Heaviside step functions for each condition\footnote{Note that this product 
formulation naturally generalizes to an arbitrary number of conditions} $H(x)$, where $H(x) = 1$ for $x > 0$ and $0$ for $x \le 0$:

$$D(y_T, y_C) = \prod_{i} H\Big(\Pr(\delta \ge q_{c,i} \mid y_T, y_C) - p_{c,i}\Big)$$ 



The specification of priors, the sample sizes ($n_T$, $n_C$), and the decision function $D(y_T, y_C)$ uniquely define a conditional Decision Boundary 
$D_T(y_C)$ for each observed control outcome $y_C$,
$$D_T(y_C) = \max\{y_T : D(y_T, y_C) = 1\}$$ 
This represents the maximal threshold the treatment data $y_T$ must satisfy to declare success.

Given this boundary, we can compute the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C)$ required in Equation \ref{eq:m_joint}. Assuming
 true parameters $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$, the overall probability of a success is the expectation of crossing the boundary $D_T(y_C)$ over the sampling 
 distribution of the control data:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_general}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} F_T\left(D_T(y_C) \mid \theta_T\right) f_C\left(y_C \mid \theta_C\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
where $F_T\left(\cdot \mid \theta_T\right)$ is the cumulative distribution function of the treatment statistic evaluated at the decision boundary, and
 $f_C\left(\cdot \mid \theta_C\right)$ is the probability density function of the control statistic.

Because we are working with normally distributed endpoints, the sampling distributions for the sample means are normal: 
$y_m \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_m^2)$ for $m \in \{T, C\}$, where $SEM_m = \sigma_m / \sqrt{n_m}$. Consequently, the general integral in 
Equation \ref{eq:oc2S_general} can be evaluated directly using the standard normal CDF ($\Phi$) and PDF ($\phi$):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_normal}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Phi\left(\frac{D_T(y_C) - \theta_T}{SEM_T}\right) \frac{1}{SEM_C} \phi\left(\frac{y_C - \theta_C}{SEM_C}\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
 

\subsection{The Experimental Unit Information Index}
\label{subsec:euii}


Now we will extend the Experimental Unit Information
Index (EUII), recently introduced by \cite{held2025balancing}, to
the Bayesian borrowing framework.


\paragraph{The Diagnostic Odds Ratio}
The diagnostic odds ratio (DOR) is a summary of a binary test's discriminatory performance borrowed 
from the diagnostic accuracy literature \citep{glas2003diagnostic}. For a 
statistical hypothesis test with T1E rate $\alpha$ and 
power $1 - \beta$, the DOR is defined as the ratio of the odds 
of rejection under the alternative to the odds of rejection under 
the null:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_dor}
    \DOR 
    = \frac{(1-\beta)/\beta}{\alpha/(1-\alpha)}
    = \frac{\Pow/(1-\Pow)}
           {\TIE/(1-\TIE)}.
\end{equation}
%
A $\DOR > 1$ indicates that the odds of a positive 
decision are higher under the alternative than under the null, 
which holds for any test with power exceeding its T1E 
rate. The DOR increases with power and decreases with T1E inflation. 

The classical EUII of \cite{held2025balancing} normalizes the DOR
by the sample size $n_{\text{trial}} = n_C + n_T$ to obtain a per-unit measure of
evidentiary efficiency:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_euii}
    \EUII = \DOR^{1/n_{\text{trial}}}.
\end{equation}
%
This can be interpreted as the geometric mean contribution of a 
single experimental unit to the total discriminatory evidence of 
the design. A value $\EUII > 1$ indicates that each 
additional unit increases the odds of correctly distinguishing 
the alternative from the null on average.

To quantify the per patient information yield, we define the Average EUII ($\EUII_{avg}$) from the averaged Power and T1E. The Averaged DOR is
$$\DOR_{avg}(\delta) = \frac{\avgPow(\delta) / (1 - \avgPow(\delta))}{\avgTIE / (1 - \avgTIE)},$$
and normalizing by the trial sample size $n_{\text{trial}}$ yields
$$\EUII_{avg}(\delta) = \left[ \DOR_{avg}(\delta) \right]^{\frac{1}{n_{\text{trial}}}}.$$

As with the underlying metrics, the Conditional EUII ($\EUII_{cond}$) is the special case obtained when $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ degenerates to a Dirac point mass at $\theta_C^*$: replacing the averaged metrics by the conditional $\Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta)$ and $\TIE(\theta_C^*)$ gives the pointwise $\DOR_{cond}(\theta_C^*, \delta)$ and $\EUII_{cond}(\theta_C^*, \delta)$, which measure how much information each patient provides when the true control value is exactly $\theta_C^*$.

\Remark For a non-degenerate design prior, the average EUII is \emph{not} the design prior expectation of the pointwise DOR:
$$\DOR_{avg}(\delta) \neq \int \DOR_{cond}(\theta_C, \delta) \pi_D(\theta_C) d\theta_C,$$
because the ratio of expectations does not, in general, equal the expectation of a ratio. 


\paragraph{Which sample size to use}

The definition in Equation~\eqref{eq:classical_euii} leaves open a question that is crucial under Bayesian borrowing: what is $n$?

The robust MAP analysis prior blends an informative MAP component with a vague component, controlled by a robustness weight $\omega \in [0, 1]$: at $\omega = 0$ the prior reduces to the full MAP prior and at $\omega = 1$ it becomes entirely vague. The effective sample size $n_{\text{A}}$ of the analysis prior therefore depends on $\omega$: it is largest for the full MAP ($\omega = 0$) and shrinks to zero for the vague prior ($\omega = 1$). One might therefore argue for a penalized denominator $n_{\text{eff}} = n_{\text{trial}} + n_{\text{A}}$, attributing part of the evidentiary gain to the historical patients.

We argue that this is the wrong normalisation. The EUII is a design tool: it answers how much evidence each newly enrolled patient contributes. Historical patients are already given. They have been collected and encoded in the prior before the current trial begins. Including $n_{\text{A}}$ in the denominator conflates two separate decisions: the prior choice (which $\omega$ to use) and the trial design (how many new patients to enroll). This conflation is particularly misleading because a more informative prior (low $\omega$, high $n_{\text{A}}$) would be penalised in the denominator for the very information it provides, while a vague prior ($\omega = 1$, $n_{\text{A}} = 0$) would not. The denominator should reflect the quantity the investigator actually controls.

We therefore use throughout:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:euii_avg}
    \EUII = \left[ \DOR_{\text{avg}} \right]^{1 / n_{\text{trial}}}
\end{equation}
where $n_{\text{trial}}$ is the number of patients enrolled in the current trial, which is the decision variable of the trial design.

\textbf{Running example.}
In Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii}, EUII is plotted for the running example. The left panel shows that Average EUII provides a more conservative value than Conditional EUII.
The right panel shows the Average EUII as a function of $\sigma_{D,C}$.

\Remark For ease of notation, from now on the EUII refers to the average EUII; the conditional EUII is the particular case obtained with a Dirac design prior.

<<plot_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="EUII for the running example. Left: Conditional and Average EUII across $\\delta$ under aligned priors. Right: Average EUII at $\\delta = 60$ versus design uncertainty $\\sigma_{D,C}$; the dotted line marks alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- EUII Data & Plots ---
df_euii_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    Cond_EUII = calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_SC), N_total),
    Avg_EUII  = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) %>% pivot_longer(cols = c(Cond_EUII, Avg_EUII), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_euii_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_EUII = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 60, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0, k_SC), N_total))

p3a <- ggplot(df_euii_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) + scale_color_manual(values = c("Avg_EUII" = "#009E73", "Cond_EUII" = "#CC79A7")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII") + my_theme

p3b <- ggplot(df_euii_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_EUII)) +
  geom_line(color = "#009E73", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1.0, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]), y = expression(EUII[avg])) + my_theme

grid.arrange(p3a, p3b, ncol = 2)
@

\paragraph{Asymptotic value} 
Under the closed-form (single Normal) model, the EUII of the SC design converges to a finite asymptotic value: holding the allocation ratio $r_T = n_T/n_C$ fixed and letting $n_C \to \infty$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:euii_asymptotic}
  \lim_{n_C \to \infty} \EUII(\delta)
  = \exp\!\left[\frac{r_T\big[(\delta - q)^2 + q^2\big]}{2\sigma^2 (1 + r_T)^2}\right],
\end{equation}
Explicit derivations are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:app_asymptotic}. 

\Remark This value is identical to the one presented in \citet{held2025balancing} by setting $r_T = 1$, $\sigma = 1$ and $q = 0$.

Importantly, the asymptotic value is independent of the analysis and design priors: Bayesian borrowing doesn't govern this value, but only how quickly it is reached. Moreover, 
the allocation ratio enters only through $r_T/(1 + r_T)^2$, which is maximised by a balanced design ($r_T = 1$). Because the value arises from the prior contribution disappearing as $n_C \to \infty$, the same limit 
is expected for the robust and mixture analysis priors as well, even though the closed form assumes a single Normal prior. \note{Maybe evaluate numerically in the mixture MAP case}

\subsection{Borrowing on the Treatment Contrast}
\label{subsec:contrast_limitation}

All the metrics developed above (average T1E, average power, and the EUII) assume borrowing on the control arm. Historical information could instead be placed directly on the treatment contrast $\delta$. We restrict the present work to borrowing on the control arm because the metrics do not transfer cleanly to that setting. We outline the three obstacles below.

\paragraph{The prior conflicts with the null}
A prior placed on $\delta$ typically favours a beneficial effect, which directly conflicts with the null hypotesis. This conflict inflates the classical T1E and it has been shown that requiring strict frequentist control of it forces the historical information to be
 discarded altogether \citep{kopp-schneider_power_2020, psioda_bayesian_2019}.

\paragraph{No average T1E analogue}
The results of this manuscript rely on the fact that setting the analysis prior equal to the design prior allow exact control of the avgT1E when borrowing on the control arm. \citet{psioda_bayesian_2019} apply the same idea to contrast borrowing, integrating the conditional rejection probability over a null design prior, 
defined as the analysis prior truncated and normalized to $\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}$. This can be calibrated to a target level, but only when that null prior places sufficient mass near $\delta_{\text{null}}$. More fundamentally, \citet{best_beyond_2025} show that no exact analogue of the average T1E result is available 
for contrast borrowing.

\paragraph{The EUII cannot be built}
In place of the T1E, \citet{best_beyond_2025} propose a joint probability metric:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:t3e}
  \int_{\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}} \Pr(\text{Success}\mid\delta)\, p(\delta)\, d\delta \;=\; \avgTIE\times\Pr(\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}),
\end{equation}
the probability that the effect is null or harmful and the trial succeeds, also called the Type III error \citep{spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986} or pre-posterior probability of a false positive result. 
The EUII is built from the diagnostic odds ratio, whose false positive input must be a conditional rate: the probability of success given that the treatment does not work, that is, the T1E. 
The Type III error is instead a joint probability, and its extra factor $\Pr(\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}})$ reflects only how likely the null was assumed to be, not the trial's ability to control errors. 
It therefore cannot serve as a replacement for T1E.

We leave the extension of these metrics to borrowing on the treatment contrast as a direction for future work.
 

\subsection{Dual Criterion}
\label{subsec:dual_criterion_methods}
Following the DC design of Section~\ref{subsec:dual_criterion_intro}, we extend the framework so that trial success requires two posterior conditions to hold 
simultaneously: a significance criterion against the null value $q$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_general}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge p
\end{equation} 
and a clinical-relevance criterion against a decision value $\DV > q$, requiring the posterior median to be clinically meaningful:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_general_clin}
    \Pr(\delta > \DV \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.5
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Analytical results under Normal Priors for the Dual Criterion}
Under the assumption of Normality for both analysis and design priors, the Dual Criterion (DC) reduces to a single condition on the posterior mean $\hat{\delta}$: the significance criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general} is, 
as in Equation~\eqref{eq:threshold_k}, equivalent to $\hat{\delta} > q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}$, while the clinical criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general_clin} is equivalent 
to $\hat{\delta} \ge \DV$, since the Normal posterior median equals its mean. Combining the two,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:k_dc}
    \hat{\delta} > k_{\DC}, \qquad k_{\DC} = \max\!\left(\DV, \; q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}\right) = \max(\DV, k).
\end{equation}
with $V_\delta = \sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C) + v_T$. 

The decision rule is thus the only change relative to the Single Criterion (SC) design (the standard design that applies the significance criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general} alone): the estimator $\hat{\delta}$, its sampling distribution, and
its marginal distribution are unchanged. Replacing the threshold $k$ by $k_{\DC}$ in Equations~\eqref{eq:power_closed_form}-\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form} 
gives all four operating characteristics directly. For example, the average Power and T1E become
\begin{align}
    \avgPow_{\DC}(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\!\left( \frac{k_{\DC} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C}) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right), \label{eq:dc_avgpower} \\[4pt]
    \avgTIE_{\DC} &= 1 - \Phi\!\left( \frac{k_{\DC} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right). \label{eq:dc_avgt1e}
\end{align}
The conditional metrics follow by the same substitution. 
   
\Remark Since $k_{\DC} \ge k$, the DC is more conservative: $\Pow_{\DC} \le \Pow_{\SC}$ 
and $\avgPow_{\DC} \le \avgPow_{\SC}$ at fixed sample size. 
Moreover, when analysis and design priors coincide and $q = 0$, the average T1E equals the nominal $\alpha$ when $z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} \ge \DV$, and drops below $\alpha$ 
when $z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} < \DV$. The exact control of the SC is therefore preserved or tightened, never violated.

\paragraph{Sample size computation}
The sample size of a DC design must be at least $n_{\min}$, where $n_{\min}$ denotes the minimum sample size such that a point estimate at the decision value, $\hat{\delta} = \DV$, is statistically significant \citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}.
To compute $n_{\min}$, we equate the two thresholds in Equation~\eqref{eq:k_dc}, $k = \DV$, under the same assumptions used for the power calculation of Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula}: a correctly specified analysis prior, a
common known variance $\sigma^2$ in both arms ($v_C = \sigma^2/n_C$, $v_T = \sigma^2/n_T$) and an analysis prior summarised by its effective sample size
$n_A$.

We finally obtain: 
$$ \frac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \frac{1}{r_T\, n_C} = \frac{(\DV - q)^2}{\sigma^2 z_p^2} =: \frac{1}{M}, $$
which is the same equation solved for the sample size computations in the SC case, with the power constant $K = \sigma^2(z_p + z_{1-\beta})^2/(\delta-q)^2$ replaced by $M = \sigma^2 z_p^2/(\DV-q)^2$, that is, evaluated at $z_{1-\beta} = 0$ and $\delta = \DV$. As in that derivation, this gives a quadratic in $n_C$:
$$ r_T\, n_C^2 + \bigl[r_T n_A - M(r_T+1)\bigr]\, n_C - M n_A = 0, $$
whose positive root is the minimum control arm size:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_nmin_general}
    n_{C,\min} = \frac{M(r_T + 1) - r_T n_A + \sqrt{\big[M(r_T + 1) - r_T n_A\big]^2 + 4\, r_T\, M\, n_A}}{2\, r_T},
\end{equation}
identical to Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} under the replacement $K \mapsto M$. Setting $z_{1-\beta} = 0$ means targeting a $50\%$ power, so requiring the two thresholds to coincide is the same as requiring $50\%$ power at $\delta = \DV$. This result, well known for the conditional power \citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, thus extends also to the average power as well under aligned priors.
In the case of no borrowing ($n_A \to 0$) with a balanced design ($r_T = 1$), Equation~\eqref{eq:dc_nmin_general} reduces to $n_{C,\min} = 2M$ control patients and, by balance, $n_{T,\min} = 2M$ treatment patients. The total minimum sample size is therefore:
$$ n_{\min} = n_{C,\min} + n_{T,\min} = 2M + 2M = \frac{4\, z_p^2\, \sigma^2}{(\DV-q)^2}, $$
which recovers the DC formula of \citet{roychoudhury_beyond_2018} for a Normal endpoint, where the treatment in a balanced trial has variance $4\sigma^2/n_{\min}$.

 
\textbf{Running example.} 
We extend the running example with a clinical relevance criterion. In addition to the significance requirement $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.95$, success now also requires $\Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.5$, that is, a decision value $\DV = 50$. With $\sigma = 88$ and $z_p = \Phi^{-1}(0.95) = 1.645$, we obtain:
$$
    M = \frac{\sigma^2 z_p^2}{(\DV - q)^2} = \frac{88^2 \times 1.645^2}{50^2} \approx 8.38.
$$
Using the running example allocation $r_T = 2$ and historical $\mathrm{ESS}$ $n_A = 20$, the minimum sample size follows from Equation~\eqref{eq:dc_nmin_general}, giving $n_{C,\min} \approx 6.2$, so $n_{C,\min} = 7$ control patients ($n_{T,\min} = 14$ and $n_{\min}=21$). Without borrowing ($n_A \to 0$) the same requirement needs $n_{C,\min} = M(1 + 1/r_T) \approx 12.6$ control patients. 
The actual design ($n_C = 20$) is well above this minimum: $k = q + z_p\sqrt{V_\delta} \approx 32 < \DV = 50$, and the average T1E falls below nominal: $\avgTIE = 1 - \Phi(\DV/\sqrt{V_\delta}) = 1 - \Phi(50/19.7) \approx 0.6\%$ rather than $5\%$. 

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_t1e_ratio} shows, on the left, the ratio of the T1E of the DC design to that of the SC design (a value below $1$ means the relevance threshold lowers the metric) and, on the right, the absolute avgT1E of both designs. The conditional ratio is shrunk more than the averaged one, and the 
avgT1E of the DC design is controlled more uniformly: on the right panel it stays below the nominal $\alpha = 0.05$ over a wider range of true control means than the SC design.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power_ratio} shows, on the left, the conditional and average power of the DC design: as for the SC design, the average power lies closer to $50\%$ than the conditional one. On the right it shows the average power of both designs. The DC design has uniformly lower avgPower and, as expected, its power at the decision value sits exactly at $50\%$.

The EUII is plotted in Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii_ratio}. It is restricted to $\delta > \DV = 50$, since inspecting values below that treshold would not be meaningful for the DC (Power $< 50\%$). 
Noticeably, the two EUII curves cross: for effects moderately above DV the DC design has the higher EUII, but as $\delta$ grows the ratio declines, and for large effects the DC design has the lower EUII.
This happens because raising the success threshold has two opposing effects on the EUII. First, it lowers the avgT1E by a fixed amount and, since the avgT1E is computed under $\delta = 0$, this gain is the same whatever the true effect. 
Second, it lowers the avgPower, and this loss grows with $\delta$: the SC design reaches $\avgPow \approx 1$ sooner, leading to a steep increase in the avgPower odds. 
For moderate effects the fixed reduction in avgT1E outweighs the small loss of avgPower, so the DC design has higher EUII, while for large effects the avgPower loss dominates. \note{I also tried to evaluate $\EUII_{\DC} = \EUII_{\SC}$ as a function of $\delta$. This could lead to a closed form intersection value for $\delta$, but I can't solve it algebraically, so maybe there isn't one. }

<<plot_t1e_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the ratio of the DC to the SC T1E under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$, assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$), for the Conditional ratio (evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$) and the Average ratio. Right panel shows the corresponding average T1E of the two designs. The vertical gray dotted line indicates the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$, and the red dashed line the nominal value of $0.05$.">>=
# Dual-criterion threshold for the running example (k_SC defined in setup) 
DV   <- 50  
k_DC <- max(DV, k_SC)  

df_rt1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    Conditional = condPower(mu_D, 0, k_DC) / condPower(mu_D, 0, k_SC),
    Average     = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC) / avgPower(mu_D,  sigma_A2, 0, k_SC)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(Conditional, Average), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Ratio")

df_t1e_abs <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(  
     DC         = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC),
    SC = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC) 
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgT1E")

pr1a <- ggplot(df_rt1e_loc, aes(mu_D, Ratio, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average" = "#E69F00", "Conditional" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "T1E ratio (DC / SC)") + my_theme
 
pr1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_abs, aes(mu_D, avgT1E, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = "#D55E00", "SC" = "#0072B2")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "Average T1E") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr1a, pr1b, ncol = 2)
@

<<plot_power_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the Conditional and Average Power of the DC design evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows the Average Power of the DC and SC designs across the same effects. The vertical gray dotted line marks the decision value $\\DV = 50$.">>=
df_pow_dc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta, k_DC),
    `Average Power`     = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`),
               names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_abs <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    DC         = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC),
    SC = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgPower")

pr2a <- ggplot(df_pow_dc, aes(delta, Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = "#E69F00", "Conditional Power" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "Power (DC)") + my_theme

pr2b <- ggplot(df_pow_abs, aes(delta, avgPower, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = "#D55E00", "SC" = "#0072B2")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "Average Power") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr2a, pr2b, ncol = 2)
@

<<plot_euii_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the ratio of the DC to the SC EUII, for the Conditional and Average metric, evaluated across true treatment effects (aligned priors $\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows the corresponding EUII of the two designs.">>=
df_reuii_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(50, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    Conditional = calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_DC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_DC), N_total) /
                  calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_SC), N_total),
    Average     = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC), N_total) /
                  calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(Conditional, Average), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Ratio")

df_euii_abs <- data.frame(delta = seq(50, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    DC         = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC), N_total),
    SC = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgEUII")

pr3a <- ggplot(df_reuii_loc, aes(delta, Ratio, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average" = "#009E73", "Conditional" = "#CC79A7")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII ratio (DC / SC)") + my_theme

pr3b <- ggplot(df_euii_abs, aes(delta, avgEUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = "#D55E00", "SC" = "#0072B2")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr3a, pr3b, ncol = 2)
@

\paragraph{Asymptotic value}
Under the closed-form (single Normal) model, we can derive the asymptotic EUII (see Appendix~\ref{sec:app_asymptotic} for details).
\begin{equation}\label{eq:euii_asymptotic_dc}
  \lim_{n_C \to \infty} \EUII^{\DC}(\delta)
  = \exp\!\left[\frac{r_T\big[(\delta - \DV)^2 + \DV^2\big]}{2\sigma^2 (1 + r_T)^2}\right],
\end{equation}
\Remark This is the SC asymptotic value~\eqref{eq:euii_asymptotic} with $q$ replaced by DV: asymptotically the DC behaves like a superiority test against the clinical margin DV. 
Because $(\delta - \DV)^2 + \DV^2 < \delta^2$ whenever $0 < \DV < \delta$, the DC asymptotic value is strictly below the SC one, as can be seen in Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii_asymptotic}, 
where the asymptotic values are plotted for the running example. 

<<plot_euii_asymptotic, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=12, fig.cap="SC versus DC EUII for the running example. Left: asymptotic ($n_C \\to \\infty$) values as a function of $\\delta$. The DC asymptotic value is shown only for $\\delta > \\DV$. Right: EUII at $\\delta = 60$ as a function of the control size $n_C$ (log scale), with the corresponding asymptotic values as dashed lines.">>=
library(gridExtra)
sigma <- 88; rT_a <- 2; DV_a <- 50; n_A_a <- 20; z_p_a <- qnorm(0.95)
cols_euii <- c("DC" = "#D55E00", "SC" = "#0072B2")

## asymptotic value with limiting threshold kappa (q for SC, DV for DC)
euii_asymptotic <- function(delta, kappa)
  exp(rT_a * ((delta - kappa)^2 + kappa^2) / (2 * sigma^2 * (1 + rT_a)^2))

## --- Left panel: asymptotic values as a function of delta ---
dd <- seq(0, 100, length.out = 200)
df_asym <- rbind(
  data.frame(delta = dd,             Criterion = "SC", EUII = euii_asymptotic(dd, 0)),
  data.frame(delta = dd[dd >= DV_a], Criterion = "DC", EUII = euii_asymptotic(dd[dd >= DV_a], DV_a))
)
p_left <- ggplot(df_asym, aes(delta, EUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV_a, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = cols_euii) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Asymptotic EUII" ) + my_theme

## --- Right panel: finite-n EUII vs n_C at delta = 60, with asymptotes ---
Lstable <- function(x) pnorm(x, log.p = TRUE) - pnorm(x, lower.tail = FALSE, log.p = TRUE)  # logit Phi in log space

euii_finite <- function(n_C, criterion, delta = 60) {
  s <- sigma * sqrt(1 / (n_C + n_A_a) + 1 / (rT_a * n_C))   # see Formula (12). avgPower for aligned priors

  k  <- z_p_a * s                                          # SC threshold (q = 0)
  if (criterion == "DC") k <- max(k, DV_a)                 # DC threshold = max(k_SC, DV)
  exp((Lstable((delta - k) / s) - Lstable(-k / s)) / (n_C * (1 + rT_a)))
}

nc <- exp(seq(log(20), log(1e5), length.out = 200))

df_nc <- rbind(
  data.frame(n_C = nc, Criterion = "SC", EUII = sapply(nc, euii_finite, criterion = "SC")),
  data.frame(n_C = nc, Criterion = "DC", EUII = sapply(nc, euii_finite, criterion = "DC"))
)

asy <- data.frame(Criterion = c("SC", "DC"),
                  asy_value = c(euii_asymptotic(60, 0), euii_asymptotic(60, DV_a)))

p_right <- ggplot(df_nc, aes(n_C, EUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_hline(data = asy, aes(yintercept = asy_value, color = Criterion),
             linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.5, inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.1) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_color_manual(values = cols_euii) +
  labs(x = expression("Control sample size " * n[C] * " (log scale)"),
       y = expression(EUII * " (" * delta * " = 60)")) + my_theme

grid.arrange(p_left, p_right, ncol = 2)

## I tried to get this asymptotic results using RBest, but the EUII converges to 1 even in the 1 Normal case prior 
## (Analysis prior == Design prior ). Maybe it is numerical stabiity ? 
@

Note that since the asymptotic value does not depend on the analysis prior, it is reasonable to expect the MAP mixture 
and its robustified versions to reach the same asymptotic value. However, we cannot confirm this numerically, because evaluating 
a mixture of Normals relies on numerical intergration that becomes instable as $n_C \to \infty$.

\subsection{Group Sequential Design} \note{TO DO}
\label{subsec:sequential}\begin{comment}

\begin{comment}


A group sequential design (GSD) with one interim analysis extends the fixed design by allowing the trial to stop early at a pre-specified interim look, either for \emph{efficacy} (overwhelming evidence of treatment benefit) or for \emph{futility} (insufficient evidence to justify continuing). Early stopping reduces the expected number of patients required when the signal is clear, but introduces dependence between interim and final decisions that must be accounted for when evaluating operating characteristics.

\subsubsection{Design Setup}
\label{subsubsec:seq_setup}

Let $K = 2$ denote the total number of stages. Patients are recruited in two cohorts. The cumulative sample sizes for arm $m \in \{T, C\}$ at stage $k \in \{1,2\}$ are $n_{m,1} < n_{m,2}$, with $\Delta n_{m,2} = n_{m,2} - n_{m,1}$ additional patients enrolled between stages. The total planned sample size is $n_{\text{trial}} = n_{T,2} + n_{C,2}$, reached only if the trial runs to the final analysis.

At each stage $k$, the sufficient statistics are the cumulative sample means $\bar{y}_{T,k}$ and $\bar{y}_{C,k}$. Under normal sampling with known variances:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,k} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m,\; \frac{\sigma_m^2}{n_{m,k}}\right).
\end{equation*}

The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ and the vague treatment prior are updated at each stage using the cumulative arm means, yielding the stage-$k$ posterior for the treatment contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$:
\begin{equation*}
  \delta \mid \bar{y}_{C,k}, \bar{y}_{T,k} \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat{\delta}_k, V_{\delta,k}),
\end{equation*}
where $\hat{\delta}_k$ and $V_{\delta,k}$ follow from the Normal-Normal conjugacy derivations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}.

\paragraph{Stopping rules at the interim}
After the stage-1 data have been collected, two Bayesian criteria are evaluated simultaneously:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Efficacy:} the trial stops early for success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \bar{y}_{C,1}, \bar{y}_{T,1}) > p.
  \end{equation*}
  \item \textbf{Futility:} the trial stops early without success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta > q' \mid \bar{y}_{C,1}, \bar{y}_{T,1}) < p_{\text{fut}},
  \end{equation*}
  for a (possibly identical) threshold $q'$ and a futility level $p_{\text{fut}}$.
\end{itemize}
If neither rule triggers, the trial continues to stage 2 where only the efficacy criterion is re-evaluated. Every trial trajectory therefore terminates in one of four mutually exclusive outcomes: (i) efficacy stop at stage 1, (ii) futility stop at stage 1, (iii) success at stage 2, or (iv) failure at stage 2.

A key consequence of Bayesian borrowing is that the decision boundary is not fixed on a $Z$-score scale. Because the posterior at stage $k$ depends on both arm means through the analysis prior on the control, the critical treatment mean that triggers a decision is a \emph{function} of the observed control mean $\bar{y}_{C,k}$. This data-adaptive boundary is the reason why operating characteristics cannot be derived from the Brownian motion representation used in classical frequentist GSD theory \citep{jennison_group_1999}, and numerical integration over the joint distribution of arm means is required instead.

\subsubsection{Conditional Operating Characteristics}
\label{subsubsec:cond_seq}

For fixed true parameters $(\theta_C^*, \delta)$, the conditional operating characteristics are computed by numerical integration over the joint distribution of the cumulative arm means at each stage. Since normal sampling has independent increments, the interim means $(\bar{y}_{T,1}, \bar{y}_{C,1})$ and the new-cohort means at stage 2 are mutually independent, so the cumulative mean at stage 2 satisfies:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,2} = \frac{n_{m,1}\,\bar{y}_{m,1} + \Delta n_{m,2}\,\bar{y}_{m,\Delta 2}}{n_{m,2}}, \qquad \bar{y}_{m,\Delta 2} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{\Delta n_{m,2}}\right).
\end{equation*}

Integrating over the marginal distribution of $(\bar{y}_{T,1}, \bar{y}_{C,1})$ at stage 1, and over the conditional distribution of $(\bar{y}_{T,2}, \bar{y}_{C,2})$ for trials that do not stop early, yields the conditional stopping probabilities:
\begin{align*}
  P_{\text{eff},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{efficacy stop at stage 1} \mid \theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  P_{\text{fut},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{futility stop at stage 1} \mid \theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  P_{\text{cont}}(\theta_C^*, \delta)   &= 1 - P_{\text{eff},1} - P_{\text{fut},1}, \\
  P_{\text{suc},2}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{efficacy at stage 2} \mid \text{continue}, \theta_C^*, \delta) \cdot P_{\text{cont}}.
\end{align*}

The overall conditional Power and T1E are then:
\begin{align}
  \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta) &= P_{\text{eff},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta) + P_{\text{suc},2}(\theta_C^*, \delta), \label{eq:seq_power} \\
  \TIE(\theta_C^*)            &= \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta = 0). \label{eq:seq_t1e}
\end{align}

The conditional expected sample sizes, overall and conditional on the trial outcome, are:
\begin{align}
  \E[N \mid \theta_C^*, \delta]   &= n_1\!\cdot\!(P_{\text{eff},1} + P_{\text{fut},1}) + n_2\!\cdot\! P_{\text{cont}}, \label{eq:seq_en} \\
  \E[N^+ \mid \theta_C^*, \delta] &= \frac{n_1 \cdot P_{\text{eff},1} + n_2 \cdot P_{\text{suc},2}}{\Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta)}, \label{eq:seq_en_plus} \\
  \E[N^- \mid \theta_C^*, \delta] &= \frac{n_1 \cdot P_{\text{fut},1} + n_2 \cdot (P_{\text{cont}} - P_{\text{suc},2})}{1 - \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta)}, \label{eq:seq_en_minus}
\end{align}
where $n_k = n_{T,k} + n_{C,k}$ is the total sample size at stage $k$, and $N^+$ (resp.\ $N^-$) denotes the number of patients enrolled in a trial that is declared successful (resp.\ unsuccessful).

\subsubsection{Average Operating Characteristics}
\label{subsubsec:avg_seq}

Following the averaging argument of Section~\ref{sec:pointwise}, we integrate the conditional metrics over the design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$:
\begin{align}
  \avgPow(\delta) &= \int \Pow(\theta_C, \delta)\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C, \label{eq:seq_avgpower} \\
  \avgTIE           &= \avgPow(\delta = 0). \label{eq:seq_avgt1e}
\end{align}

The average expected sample sizes, conditional on trial outcome and hypothesis $H_i$ (with $\delta_i = 0$ under $H_0$ and $\delta_i = \DV$ under $H_1$), are weighted averages of the conditional expected sample sizes:
\begin{align}
  E_D[N^+ \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \E[N^+ \mid \theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot \Pow(\theta_C, \delta_i)\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{\avgPow(\delta_i)}, \label{eq:seq_avg_enp} \\[6pt]
  E_D[N^- \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \E[N^- \mid \theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot (1-\Pow(\theta_C, \delta_i))\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{1-\avgPow(\delta_i)}. \label{eq:seq_avg_enm}
\end{align}

Because the data-adaptive Bayesian boundary precludes closed-form evaluation, the integrals in Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_avgpower}--\eqref{eq:seq_avg_enm} are computed numerically.

\subsubsection{EUII for Sequential Designs}
\label{subsubsec:seq_euii}

In a sequential design, the sample size $N$ is random. A trial that stops early at stage 1 enrolls fewer patients than one that runs to the final analysis. \citet{held2025balancing} generalise the EUII to accommodate this by replacing the fixed exponent $1/n_{\text{trial}}$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:classical_euii} with outcome-specific effective information per patient:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:seq_euii}
  \EUII_{\text{seq}} = \frac{(\text{LR}^+)^{\E(1/N^+)}}{(\text{LR}^-)^{\E(1/N^-)}},
\end{equation}
where $\text{LR}^+ = \avgPow/\avgTIE$ and $\text{LR}^- = (1-\avgPow)/(1-\avgTIE)$ are the likelihood ratios of a positive and a negative outcome respectively, computed using the average metrics of Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_avgpower}--\eqref{eq:seq_avgt1e}.

The exponents $\E(1/N^+)$ and $\E(1/N^-)$ are the expected inverse sample sizes, mixing across hypotheses according to their posterior probability given the trial outcome \citep{held2025balancing}:
\begin{align}
  \E(1/N^+) &= E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_0]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{success}) + E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_1]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{success}), \label{eq:seq_einvnp} \\
  \E(1/N^-) &= E_D[1/N^- \mid H_0]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{no success}) + E_D[1/N^- \mid H_1]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{no success}). \label{eq:seq_einvnm}
\end{align}

The hypothesis-specific expected inverse sample sizes are obtained by averaging $\E[1/N^\pm \mid \theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot\Pr(\text{outcome}\mid\theta_C, \delta_i)$ over the design prior and then normalising:
\begin{align}
  E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \E[1/N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot\Pow(\theta_C, \delta_i)\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{\avgPow(\delta_i)}, \label{eq:seq_inv_enp} \\[6pt]
  E_D[1/N^- \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \E[1/N^-\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot(1-\Pow(\theta_C, \delta_i))\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{1-\avgPow(\delta_i)}. \label{eq:seq_inv_enm}
\end{align}
Note that $\E[1/N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]\neq 1/\E[N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]$ in general (Jensen's inequality). For a two stage design with cumulative sample sizes $n_1$ and $n_2$, the integrand in~\eqref{eq:seq_inv_enp} decomposes across stages as
\begin{equation}
  \E[1/N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot\Pow(\theta_C, \delta_i)
  = \frac{P_{\text{eff},1}(\theta_C, \delta_i)}{n_1} + \frac{P_{\text{suc},2}(\theta_C, \delta_i)}{n_2},
\end{equation}
and analogously for the failure arm, where $P_{\text{eff},1}$ is the stage-1 efficacy-stopping probability and $P_{\text{suc},2}$ is the probability of reaching and succeeding at the final analysis.
The posterior probabilities $\Pr(H_0 \mid \cdot)$ and $\Pr(H_1 \mid \cdot)$ in Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_einvnp}--\eqref{eq:seq_einvnm} are determined by the prior probability $\Pr(H_1)$ and the DOR via Bayes' theorem.

When the design never stops early, $\E[N^+ \mid \theta_C, \delta] = \E[N^- \mid \theta_C, \delta] = n_{\text{trial}}$ for all $\theta_C$, so $\E(1/N^+) = \E(1/N^-) = 1/n_{\text{trial}}$ and Equation~\eqref{eq:seq_euii} reduces exactly to the fixed design formula~\eqref{eq:euii_avg}. Under an effective sequential design, efficacy stopping at stage 1 concentrates $N^+$ at $n_1 < n_{\text{trial}}$, so $\E(1/N^+) > 1/n_{\text{trial}}$. Symmetrically, futility stopping raises $\E(1/N^-)$ above $1/n_{\text{trial}}$. Both effects increase $\EUII_{\text{seq}}$ relative to the fixed design, making sequential monitoring attractive not only for its patient-saving properties but also for the per patient evidentiary yield it achieves.
\end{comment}

\section{Applications}
\label{sec:Applications}
<<load.functions, include=FALSE, cache=FALSE>>=
source("../scripts/prior_on_control/00.functions.MC.R")
library(xtable)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(RBesT)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)  
library(ggplot2) 
@  
 
\subsection{Application 1: Crohn's disease}
\label{sec:application1}

We evaluate the design of a Proof-of-Concept (PoC) study in Crohn’s disease, an inflammatory bowel disease. The primary endpoint is the change from baseline to week 6 in the Crohn’s Disease Activity Index (CDAI), a 
continuous measure where lower scores indicate clinical improvement.

The original trial evaluated the efficacy of secukinumab against a placebo \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}. 
Let $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$ be the true underlying mean changes for the control and treatment arms, so that the treatment effect is $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, with $\delta > 0$ representing a treatment benefit.


To improve trial efficiency, an informative analysis prior ($\pi_A$) for the placebo arm was derived from 671
historical placebo patients across six previous Crohn’s disease trials via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach.



\paragraph{Decision Rules}
At any given analysis, a successful terminal decision requires both strong evidence of a positive treatment effect and sufficient evidence of a clinically relevant 
effect size:
$$
\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.50
$$

where $\mathcal{D}$ denotes the observed data up to that point. Furthermore, sequential designs in this application incorporate a Bayesian futility criterion. 
The trial can be stopped for futility if it is highly probable that the treatment effect is less than 40:

$$
\Pr(\delta < 40 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.90
 $$



\paragraph{Design Variations and Evaluation Strategy}
The literature presents varying implementations of this trial. The original protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab} planned two interim analyses, occurring when 17 and 41 patients, 
respectively, reached the 6 week evaluation time point. Conversely, \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} modified this into a two stage group sequential design with a 1:2 allocation 
ratio (10 placebo and 20 experimental patients per stage). Taking a simpler approach, \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluated the trial as a fixed design using 20 and 40 
individuals for the control and treatment arms, omitting the dual success criterion. Another cause of divergence is terminal decision: while \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} 
allowed a final "indeterminate" result by evaluating futility at the final analysis, we follow the original formulation, which restricts the final stage to a 
binary outcome (success or failure) by removing the terminal futility check.



Therefore, we will compute $\avgTIE$, $\avgPow$, and $\EUII$ across three design variations of this trial:
 \begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Fixed Design:} A single-stage design (analogous to the evaluation by \citet{best_beyond_2025}). \footnote{\citet{best_beyond_2025} originally used a threshold of $0.975$. We adopt $0.95$ throughout for consistency with the other Design Variations.}
  
  \item \textbf{One Interim Analysis:} An evaluation of the two stage group-sequential design proposed by \citet{gsponer_practical_2014}.
  \item \textbf{Two Interim Analyses:} An evaluation of the original three-stage trial protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}.

\end{enumerate}






 
\paragraph{Analysis Priors ($\pi_A$)}
The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ determines the degree of historical information incorporated into the final posterior decision:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Normal MAP:} A single-component Normal prior obtained using the MAP approach on historical data to evaluate closed form results: $\pi_{\text{Normal}}(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(-50,\, 88^2/20)$: 
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A mixture of three Normals obtained using the MAP approach: 
  $\pi_{\text{MAP}}(\theta_C) = 0.48\,\mathcal{N}(-52.5,\,21.2^2) + 0.46\,\mathcal{N}(-47.5,\,7.8^2) + 0.06\,\mathcal{N}(-50.4,\,48.2^2)$
  \item \textbf{Robust MAP ($\omega \in \{0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8\}$):} Four robust mixture priors constructed by assigning a weight $\omega$ to a non-informative component and $(1-\omega)$ to the MAP 3-component prior. 
  The mean of the vague component is set to $-50$ to be consistent with the historical data, and its standard deviation is set to $\sigma = 88$, to be approximately equivalent to one subject's 
  worth of information. The value of $\omega$ reflects our prior belief about the possibility of non-exchangeability between the placebo effect from the historical studies and the 
  placebo effect in the new study.
  \item \textbf{Vague:} A non-informative prior representing a design with no historical borrowing, serving as the frequentist baseline: $\pi_{\text{vague}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-50,\,8800^2)$. The standard deviation is set to $8800 = 100\,\sigma$, so the prior carries an effective sample size of $\sigma^2/8800^2 = 10^{-4}$, that is, negligible information; the design therefore reduces to the frequentist no-borrowing baseline. This is the same vague prior adopted by \citet{best_beyond_2025}.
\end{enumerate}

 

\paragraph{Design Priors ($\pi_D$)}
The design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ represents the assumed true data generating process for the control arm ($\theta_C$):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dirac:} A Dirac measure at $\theta_C^* = -50$, it recovers the classical conditional T1E and Power.
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A design prior exactly matching the corresponding 3-Component MAP analysis prior.
  \item \textbf{Skeptical:} Following the terminology of \citet{best_beyond_2025}, this prior corresponds to the posterior distribution of a stand-alone analysis of the historical study with the “most extreme” placebo effect ($\bar{y} = -90$, $n = 25$, so $\sigma/\sqrt{n} = 88/5 = 17.6$): $\pi_{\text{skep}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,17.6^2)$. Because the placebo is exceptionally effective, this scenario is highly "skeptical" of the experimental treatment's ability to demonstrate superiority.
  \item \textbf{Misspecified:} A hypothetical prior-data conflict scenario where the true control arm performs much worse than historically predicted: $\pi_{\text{miss}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-10,\,25^2)$.
\end{enumerate}




\subsubsection{Fixed Design}
\label{sec:fixed_design}
Following \citet{best_beyond_2025}, we fix the sample size at $n_C = 20$ and $n_T = 40$ and we first analyze the Single Criterion (SC) design, then the Dual Criterion (DC).
Because no closed form exists for the mixture case, operating characteristics are computed numerically using \texttt{RBesT::oc2S} and a numerical integration routine for the averaged metrics.

\paragraph{Single Criterion}

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_cond} shows the conditional T1E ($\delta = 0$, left) and conditional Power at $\delta = 60$ (right) as functions of the fixed true control mean $\theta_C^*$.
The vague prior yields the flat frequentist baseline: T1E is $0.05$ everywhere and power is determined solely by the sample size. As historical borrowing increases (decreasing $\omega$), T1E inflates with increasing severity when $\theta_C^* \ll -50$ (skeptical scenario) and is increasingly suppressed well below $\alpha$ when $\theta_C^* \gg -50$ (misspecified scenario). Robust MAP priors exhibit a monotone moderation: 
as $\omega$ increases, the curve is pulled toward the flat vague baseline, decreasing the T1E at the cost of reduced Power. 

<<plot_mix_cond, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Conditional metrics for all six analysis priors as a function of the fixed true control mean $\\theta_C^*$. Left: conditional T1E ($\\delta = 0$). Right: conditional Power at $\\delta = 60$. The Vague prior (dashed) provides the no borrowing reference. The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_cond   <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_cond.RDS")
df_cond    <- res_cond$df_cond
prior_names <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                 "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
df_cond$Prior <- factor(df_cond$Prior, levels = prior_names)
mu_A <- -50

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_cond_T1E <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = T1E, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = "Conditional T1E") +
  my_theme

p_cond_Power <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = Power, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = bquote("Conditional Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_cond_T1E, p_cond_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{Average Metrics: }
By marginalizing the conditional curves over a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, we obtain the Average T1E and Average Power for each of the 24 analysis$\times$design prior combinations.
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_avg} displays on the $x$-axis the design prior (representing different assumptions about the true data generating process) and each line as a given analysis prior.
When the analysis and design prior are aligned (MAP analysis, MAP design), avgT1E is at the nominal $0.05$, consistent with the theory. Under the skeptical design prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -90$), more informative analysis priors lead to larger avgT1E inflation. The misspecified prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -10$) drives the opposite effect: more informative prior overcorrects the control mean downward, suppressing both avgT1E and Average Power simultaneously, making the trial conservative but underpowered.
Note that the Dirac design prior serves as the best-case reference where the truth is deterministic.


<<plot_mix_avg, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average T1E (left) and Average Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) for all combinations of analysis prior and design prior. Each line connects values for a single analysis prior across the four design prior scenarios.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_avg      <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_avg <- res_avg$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_avg_T1E <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_avg, delta == 0),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgT1E,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior", y = "Average T1E") +
  my_theme

p_avg_Power <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_avg, delta == 60),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgPower,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior",
       y = bquote("Average Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_avg_T1E, p_avg_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{EUII: }
As already noted in Section~\ref{subsec:euii}, from here on we write EUII for the average EUII ($\EUII_{avg}$). Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii} plots the EUII at $\delta = 60$ against the robustness weight $\omega$, one curve per design prior. Under the aligned design prior (MAP), EUII is strictly decreasing in $\omega$ and stays above the vague baseline: when the prior is correct, borrowing genuinely improves per patient information and the metric behaves as intended.

The case of prior conflict requires carefulness. For example, the misspecified prior attains almost the same EUII as the correct one, this happens cause it suppresses the avgT1E to about $1\%$, which inflates the diagnostic odds ratio, while its average Power collapses to about $65\%$ (against $93\%$ for the aligned prior and $80\%$ for the vague one).
The skeptical prior shows the opposite distortion, and its curve is not monotone in $\omega$. At full borrowing ($\omega = 0$) its avgT1E is inflated to about $21\%$, but its avgPower is the highest of all ($97\%$), and the resulting large power odds keep the EUII above the vague baseline. Already at $\omega = 0.2$, the power decreases while the avgT1E is still inflated (about $15\%$), so the EUII drops to its minimum, below the vague baseline. 

The EUII is thus a measure that can be used as sensitivity analysis by stakeholders, but is intended to be read alongside avgPower and avgT1E, not replace them.

<<plot_mix_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII at $\\delta = 60$ as a function of the robustness weight $\\omega$, one line per design prior. The dashed line marks the Vague (no borrowing) baseline, reached by all design priors at $\\omega = 1$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

df_euii_60 <- dplyr::filter(df_euii, delta == 60)
euii_vague <- dplyr::filter(df_euii_60, Analysis_Prior == "Vague") |>
  dplyr::pull(EUII) |> unique() # all values are equal 1.074947 since it is the classic EUII w no borrowing 

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii_60,
       aes(x = omega, y = EUII,
           color = Design_Prior, group = Design_Prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = euii_vague, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.2)) +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("Robustness weight (" * omega * ")"),
       y = expression("EUII (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii_delta} shows the EUII as a function of $\delta$ for all 24 analysis prior/design prior combinations. When priors are aligned 
(both MAP), EUII rises monotonically and the ordering is strict: more borrowing (smaller $\omega$) always dominates, confirming that historical information strictly increases EUII when the prior is correctly specified. 
The Dirac design prior, which fixes the truth at the prior mean, acts as the upper bound.

<<plot_mix_euii_delta, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII as a function of the treatment effect $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. Color encodes the analysis prior (Vague shown dashed).">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii,
       aes(x = delta, y = EUII,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta), y = "EUII") +
  my_theme
@


\paragraph{Dual Criterion}

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_cond} shows the conditional T1E and conditional Power at $\delta = 60$ as functions of $\theta_C^*$.
Compared to Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_cond}, the overall pattern is qualitatively similar but shifted downward.
The T1E is uniformly lower: requiring both criterion to hold, makes it harder to declare success under the null.
As noted in Section~\ref{subsec:dual_criterion_methods}, power equals about $50\%$ exactly at the boundary $\delta = \DV = 50$, where the posterior median falls on either side of the DV with equal probability. 



<<plot_dual_cond, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="DC design: Conditional T1E (left) and conditional Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) as functions of the fixed true control mean $\\theta_C^*$, for all six analysis priors. The dashed red horizontal line marks $\\alpha = 0.05$. The Vague prior (dashed line) is the no borrowing reference. The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_dual_cond <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_cond.RDS")
df_dual_cond  <- res_dual_cond$df_cond
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
df_dual_cond$Prior <- factor(df_dual_cond$Prior, levels = prior_names)
mu_A <- -50

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_d_cond_T1E <- ggplot(df_dual_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = T1E, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "red", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = "Conditional T1E") +
  my_theme

p_d_cond_Power <- ggplot(df_dual_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = Power, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = bquote("Conditional Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_d_cond_T1E, p_d_cond_Power, ncol = 2)
@

\textbf{Average Metrics:}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_avg} shows the average T1E and average Power at $\delta = 60$ across analysis and design prior combinations.
The structure mirrors the SC results: avgT1E inflates under skeptical design priors and is suppressed under misspecified ones, while 
the MAP analysis prior aligned with the MAP design prior recovers a smaller T1E than nominal.
The DC keeps the average T1E far below the nominal $0.05$ in almost every scenario. This supports its use in PoC phase II trials, where the cost of a false positive is particularly high. 
The average Power at $\delta = 60$, mirrors the average T1E structure: it is high under the skeptical prior, moderate under the aligned and Dirac priors, and low under the misspecified prior, getting progressively 
closer to the Vague baseline as $\omega \to 1$. 

<<plot_dual_avg, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="DC design: average T1E (left) and average Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) for all 24 analysis$\\times$design prior combinations. The dashed red horizontal line marks $\\alpha = 0.05$. The Vague prior (dashed line) is the no borrowing reference">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_dual_avg  <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_avg.RDS")
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names  <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_dual_avg   <- res_dual_avg$df_avg |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_d_avg_T1E <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_dual_avg, delta == 0),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgT1E,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "red", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior", y = "Average T1E") +
  my_theme

p_d_avg_Power <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_dual_avg, delta == 60),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgPower,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior",
       y = bquote("Average Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_d_avg_T1E, p_d_avg_Power, ncol = 2)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_power_delta} shows how the average Power varies with $\delta$ above DV. 
At $\delta = DV$ the Power is close to $50\%$ only when the analysis prior is well specified (MAP and Dirac design priors), while prior-data conflict shifts it up under the skeptical prior and down under the misspecified prior.

<<plot_dual_power_delta, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="DC design: average Power as a function of the true treatment effect $\\delta$, shown for $\\delta \\geq \\text{DV} = 50$ and faceted by design prior (coloured by analysis prior). The red horizontal line marks $50\\%$ power. The Vague prior (dashed line) is the no borrowing reference.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_dual_avg  <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_avg.RDS")
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names  <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_dual_avg   <- res_dual_avg$df_avg |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_dual_avg, delta >= 50),
       aes(x = delta, y = avgPower,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, color = "red", linewidth = 0.5) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Treatment Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Average Power") +
  my_theme
@ 

\textbf{EUII:}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_euii} shows the EUII of the DC design as a function of $\delta$ for all 24 analysis prior/design prior combinations, faceted by design prior.
Under the two aligned design priors (Dirac and MAP), the more the prior borrows, the higher the EUII. 
The Skeptical and Misspecified design prior behave differently, and the reason is that the EUII is essentially a ratio of odds. A design prior that disagrees with the analysis prior biases the avgPower and the avgT1E in the same direction, 
so their odds ratio stays approximately constant. Infact, the Skeptical prior inflates both quantities while the Misspecified prior does the opposite. In both cases the EUII results in a similar value,
even though one design is badly optimistic and the other badly underpowered. 

\note{I think from here there are two ways to develop the discussion on EUII:} 
\begin{itemize}
  \item Keep considering the EUII as a "sensitivity analysis" tool to be read alongside avgPower and avgT1E to see how the design behaves under prior misspecification.
  \item Stop considering EUII useful in case of prior misspecification. In fact, the EUII seems to be a better tool for comparing designs under the assumption of correctly specified priors but its interpretation under misspecification is a bit unclear. 
  Thus, the idea would be to suggest the use of avgPower and avgT1E to perform a sensitivity analysis and evaluate the robustness of the borrowing (also evaluating different $\omega$) .
  Then, the EUII would only be used to compare differnt design choices (SC vs DC vs Sequential vs Sequential with DC and or futility). 
\end{itemize} 
For the moment, I am more towards the second but I will make the decision after the inspection vs Sequential case. 


<<plot_dual_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="DC design: EUII as a function of the treatment effect $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. Color encodes the analysis prior (Vague shown dashed).">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::filter(delta >= 50) |>   # DC EUII not meaningful below the DV
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii,
       aes(x = delta, y = EUII,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta), y = "EUII") +
  my_theme
@


<<plot_dual_euii_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Ratio $\\EUII_{\\DC} / \\EUII_{\\SC}$ of the DC to the SC EUII as a function of $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. Color encodes the analysis prior (Vague shown dashed).">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_ratio    <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_vs_FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_ratio <- res_ratio$df_ratio |>
  dplyr::filter(delta >= 50) |>   # DC EUII not meaningful below the DV
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )
 
my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_ratio,
       aes(x = delta, y = EUII_ratio,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta),
       y = bquote("EUII"["DC"] / "EUII"["SC"])) +
  my_theme
@


